The PR replaces the old hand-rolled `where_in` (which used a naive `value.replace(mark, mark*2)` quoting helper) with `WhereInMacro`, which compiles each value through SQLAlchemy's `bindparam(...).compile(dialect=..., compile_kwargs={"literal_binds": True})`. This is the dialect-aware, correct literal-escaping path (the test confirms `where_in(["O'Malley's"]) == "('O''Malley''s')"`), and the `mark` parameter is now ignored (only emits a warning comment). The values are user-controllable (Jinja template arguments) and the rendered string is executed as raw SQL, but the escaping is now done by SQLAlchemy's dialect compiler rather than a hand-written helper, so this is a fix, not a candidate flaw.

